00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
well let's get more now on brexit talks  as the foreign secretary tells sky news  that sunday will be a point of  finality for brexit trade talks if the  eu does not move  substantially in negotiations last night  boris johnson shared a three-hour dinner  with european commission president  ursula van der leyen in the hope of  breaking  months of deadlock well i'm joined now  by nigel farage uh leader of the brexit  party but  is it the brexit party or is it reform  uk now mr farage  well we've applied to the electoral  commission to change the name to reform  uk and we're awaiting an answer which we  expect by next wednesday so i think  technically you're quite right to say  brexit party for now  okay um so we heard from us  in the last few minutes really last half  hour we've also heard from both  the uk and the eu that they have no deal  contingency plans in place  uh that will allow planes to continue  flying potentially  keep fishing going are we now in a  position where no deal looks  worse than a bad deal well let me just  say that the contingency planning from  the european commission on fishing  is that things carry on the same next  year as they have this year which would  mean  the french getting 90 percent of the  haddock quota in the southwest so i  think that's actually  a huge insult yet again  the position is very simple the european  union  will not change their position why  because in october last year  boris johnson went to brussels and  signed something that we in this country  call the withdrawal agreement  but which they call the new eu treaty  and treaties in brussels are are rather  like the bible  um and they're not gonna budge and so  boris johnson  having signed up to a set of wholly  unsuitable conditions but he was able to  sell it in the uk because we were  suffering from brexit and  here he was it's an oven ready deal it's  all going to be great  and then when he got to the general  election and he was under pressure  he then effectively contradicted what  he'd signed a few weeks before  so this is the mess we're in after four  and a half  years of a conservative prime minister  and boris johnson's choice now on sunday  if indeed that  is the deadline although one does notice  that word is a fairly movable feast  but if sunday is the deadline boris  johnson's choice is simple  either he goes along with what he signed  in october last year in brussels all we  do walk away  um it's all his fault then is it  well i'm not saying it's all his fault  but i think that the european union  uh you know from the start have never  really  wanted to talk about a free trade  agreement indeed you know  even on wednesday this week ursula von  de leon was saying i look forward to  meeting boris johnson  to discuss our partnership agreement in  capital letters  they don't want a simple free trade  agreement and i'm afraid what boris  signed up to  wasn't a simple free trade agreement if  boris johnson wanted  a canada star relationship he should  never have signed  what he called his oven ready deal so so  there is fault on both sides  when you look at what potentially could  happen at the end of this year we  already know that there are big  delays at the ports uh real problems  there we know that lots of  lorry drivers have returned to their  homes in eastern europe now that that is  partly due to covid not entirely due to  brexit  uh but there is huge concern from all  sorts of industries from the transport  industry we saw  uh a report out at the weekend from the  cabinet office that was leaked that  showed that fuel prices and food prices  inevitably will go up  in in the months following do you feel  any  sense of remorse do you feel that  perhaps if you hadn't started all of  this  we wouldn't be in this position no we  should have left four years ago  we should have left four years ago and  whatever short-term bumps in the road  there may or may not have been  should have been dealt with then i i  think it's been happening there are  people who are worried about losing  their livelihoods and and it's been a  heck of a year for all of us hasn't it  with covert  this is more than just on the road for  so many people it certainly has  that there is no question look when it  comes to transport chaos kent is used to  that  operation stack with 30 mile cues on the  m20  i'm afraid it's a common occurrence uh  because we get gales in the channel or  we get the french fishermen protesting  we are used to that but we are living we  are living in the 21st century  where any any sort of customs  declaration can simply be done  on an iphone you can see that on the  american  canadian border it's not that difficult  when it comes to businesses that would  suffer a short-term hit  on tariffs well look at it this way if  the eu charges us tariffs  and we charge them tariffs we'll be  better off  to the tune of seven eight nine billion  pounds a year  so we will be able in the short term to  help anybody that is disadvantaged but  remember this  all the terrible predictions economic  predictions about what would go wrong  if we did if we even dared to vote  brexit none of those  have come true any short-term problem is  of course dwarf  anyway by the pandemic and by being free  of the single market by being able to  set our own regulation  i'm 100 confident that that's the path  to a more prosperous britain  in the next few years but the problem is  we haven't really left yet have we  the deadline hasn't really been reached  so how do we know  well we were told that even a vote to  leave would lead to economic disaster  which very clearly it did not  um and we've got to remember we've got  to remember  that the eurozone is only fifteen  percent  one five percent of global  gdp there is a great big one there's  only one fishing is only  less than one percent of ours so why do  you keep going on about it so much  because we voted to take back control of  our laws  and our borders and parts of that of  course are our territorial waters it may  be small  in economic terms but in terms of  symbolism it's very powerful indeed  um you've tried repeatedly to stand as  an mp and failed you tried a new kp  tribe at the brexit party  um do you think you're going to have any  success in with reform uk  do you know what i'm the only person you  will ever speak to who has won two  national elections leading two different  parties never in british history has  anyone done that before which i did in  the 2014  and 19 european elections when it comes  to breaking first past the post in  westminster  it is very very difficult uh the brexit  party  as you know uh fought a by-election in  peterborough last year  we lost to labor by 600 votes uh we  fought a long court case  over postal votes a theme which is quite  familiar  in american parliaments at the moment it  is difficult  there is no question are you saying that  the the democratic system in the uk  is is just like the democratic system in  america according to trump  really i think that whether is  large-scale postal voting there will  always be fraud  of that there is absolutely no doubt  anybody going to look at the number of  people who've gone to prison  in the last 20 years because of it look  i'm under no illusions  that to break the close shop of british  politics with this voting system  is a very very difficult thing to do but  what i also know is that ukip  had the most profound political change  on britain i mean literally  a conservative party that was remain is  now a conservative  party that is leave and it was the  pressure of ukip over many many years  that did that so  there are lots of ways of winning in  politics without necessarily  winning the very straightforward way  winning is getting more people to vote  for you and it seems that that that's  not  you're not being able to achieve that  within a constituency it is a simple  matter of persuading  more people to take your name than to  take somebody else's  well it just doesn't work like that  because under first pass it does work  like that  no it doesn't actually because under  first pass the post we tend to vote for  the party that we dislike the least  but the point i'm making yeah i mean  smashing the first past the post system  is really really hard but the point i'm  making  is that ukip only ever won two seats  in the westminster parliament but  completely changed  british politics and the course of this  country for the next few years so  so if we get a sellout you know from  boris johnson and let's see what happens  but  already we see big concessions on  northern ireland  the acceptance we're going to stay part  of the european arrest warrant  the acceptance that the european court  of human rights and strasbourg will  still be there  if we carry on down that route and this  is a sell-out  i don't think we're just going to walk  away because we're not  okay nigel farage always interesting to  talk to you thanks very much

